# Notices

## Licensed dependencies

### animate.css — not a dependency

Animatio's preset catalogue uses the same class NAMES as animate.css, because
compatibility is the point of a parity layer and a class name is not anybody's
property. The motion is not theirs.

It used to be. The presets were generated from a pinned copy of animate.css,
keeping its keyframe percentages, easing curves and transform values and
substituting tokens for the hardcoded geometry — a derivative work, which is why
this file once carried their copyright.

`tools/author-parity.mjs` now authors the catalogue from Animatio's own motion
model: every overshoot comes from one damped-oscillator function rather than
from hand-picked stops, and the timing functions are Animatio's easing tokens,
so retuning `--an-ease` retunes the library coherently. Sixty-two of the
ninety-seven presets are authored that way. The remaining thirty-five are
two-stop fades and slides — "opacity 0 to 1" has one sensible expression and is
nobody's invention.

`test/fixtures/animate-4.1.1.css` is kept only so `npm run import:animate` can
diff the two catalogues and report which class names have drifted apart. It is
not shipped: `files` excludes it.

Animatio's own MIT notice appears in `LICENSE`, in this file, and in the banner
comment of every file in `dist/` — preserved through minification, and
`check:attribution` fails the build if it goes missing.

## Techniques credited

No license requires these — naming your sources is cheap, and it is the
difference between a library people trust and one people are suspicious of.

- **Scroll-triggered animation via style queries** — Ryan Mulligan.
  <https://ryanmulligan.dev/blog/scroll-triggered-animations-style-queries/>
  The Tier-2 latch in `src/scroll/` is this technique.

- **"Run a scroll-driven animation only once"** — Bramus Van Damme.
  <https://www.bram.us/2023/10/05/run-a-scroll-driven-animation-only-once/>
  The analysis establishing that pure CSS cannot latch a scrubbed timeline —
  which is why Animatio ships a tier ladder and an honest limits page rather
  than a `once` option that quietly does not work.

- **The gooey `feColorMatrix` alpha-crush filter** — a long-standing community
  technique, popularised through Codrops and CSS-Tricks. `#an-goo` in
  `assets/animatio-filters.svg`.

- **`backdrop-filter: url()` interoperability** — WebKit bug 245510 and W3C
  SVGWG issue 1142 are what shaped the entire surfaces module.
  <https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=245510>
  <https://github.com/w3c/svgwg/issues/1142>

